{\*\ftnsep\chftnsep}\pgndec\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Announcer}{
 [00:00:04\u8211\'9600:00:37]\line Welcome to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum, the podcast that celebrates the multiple hats we wear and the juggling acts we perform in life. Whether you're parenting kids and teens on the autism spectrum, navigating the ever-evolving healthcare industry, or building a more inclusive and impactful world, this is your space to find connection, inspiration, and growth. Together, we'll share stories, tackle challenges, and explore how innovation and inclusion create better outcomes for everyone. Because no matter the spectrum, there's always a lighter side. Now here's your host, David Kaufer.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:00:37\u8211\'9600:02:30]\line Hello again, everybody, and welcome back to Lighter Side of the Spectrum. I'm David Kaufer. Today we're talking about something I think deserves far more attention than it gets. When most people think about autism, they think about communication differences, sensory challenges, repetitive behaviors, or social interaction. But what they don't think about usually is trauma.\line Not because trauma is necessarily a part of autism itself, but because so many autistic children and adults experience repeated events that leave lasting emotional wounds. School struggles, bullying, hospitalizations, family disruption, being misunderstood, not being able to communicate, living in a world that often wasn't designed with them in mind. Those experiences change people, and today's guest understands that reality as well as anyone. Stephen Laine is a father, autism advocate, and writer whose honest Facebook posts have resonated with thousands of families, including my own.\line Over the past several months, his family has faced every parent's nightmare, including psychiatric hospitalization, repeated elopement, navigating a system that often seems unprepared to help. But through it all, Stephen has continued to share something that's incredibly rare\u8212\'97 not polished success stories, But real life, the actual fear, the exhaustion, and the hope, and ultimately what healing can look like. Stephen, welcome to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:02:30\u8211\'9600:02:31]\line Thank you for having me.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:02:31\u8211\'9600:03:05]\line Yeah, I'm really happy to have you here. I'm glad that our paths have crossed. Like I said, one of the things that struck me is just, uh, your raw honesty, uh, in terms of approaching this. You don't always see this. in our space in terms of other parents who post on Facebook and other platforms. One of the things you wrote that really grabbed my attention was this. You said, I didn't choose advocacy, advocacy chose me.\line Yeah.\line So I think every parent probably listening to this right now understands exactly what you meant, but I'd like you to talk about what it means to you and tell us about your journey?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:03:17\u8211\'9600:03:41]\line Well, okay. Um, so I will, um, kind of go back in time a little bit here. So yeah, please, we'll go. Yeah, so we're gonna go back to the start of when I went into the autism world, as you could say. And that was in 2011. Um, I was living in Oxford, Michigan. And for those of your listeners who don't know where Oxford is, that's about 40 miles north of Detroit.\line Okay.\line It's a semi-rural area. So anyways, we're noticing, my wife at the time, we were both noticing, I'm divorced right now and I'll get into that later.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:03:55\u8211\'9600:03:59]\line Yeah, but we definitely want to get into that. I know that's a big part of your story too.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:03:59\u8211\'9600:04:26]\line It is part of my story too, but I'll get into that later. So anyways, in 2011, a lot of people were going to me and my wife at the time, They were noticing certain things with Matthew, like he wasn't hitting certain milestones. He wasn't walking at the appropriate age. He wasn't communicating at all. He was just communicating with hums, as I would like to say. And he was about 3 and a half to 4 years old at the time.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:04:26\u8211\'9600:04:27]\line Okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:04:27\u8211\'9600:04:53]\line And so, you know, everyone thinks, you know, there's something going on with him. Is it hearing impairment? Is it something else? So one of the first things I do, I get his hearing tested. Came back. Yep, he came back normal. So it wasn't that. So then I enrolled him into preschool through our school district. And they're thinking autism. And I'm disagreeing with them.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:04:53\u8211\'9600:04:56]\line I was gonna say, were you thinking autism at all?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:04:56\u8211\'9600:05:25]\line No, I wasn't. No, I wasn't. No, I wasn't. And so I'm disagreeing with them. And so I'm like, well, I'm gonna go find some doctors, and I'm gonna see if I can get him, you know, see what they say. Well, I got 2 opinions, 2 different opinions. And it was the same thing, autism. And so I'm like, well, this is a done deal. And I still wasn't able to come to terms with it.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:05:25\u8211\'9600:05:26]\line And that's fine.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:05:26\u8211\'9600:05:31]\line Now, I got a younger son by the name of Aiden. At the time, he was 2 and a half.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:05:32\u8211\'9600:05:33]\line Okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:05:33\u8211\'9600:05:57]\line And I rolled him into preschool. And I remember taking him to preschool first day. And he's not communicating with any of the peers there. He's just stacking blocks upon blocks upon blocks, perfect alignment, and humming to the blocks. Now, at this point, I already did a little bit of homework and knew what the signs are for autism at this point.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:05:58\u8211\'9600:06:37]\line Right. And I'm like, oh, here we go again. So I guess he's gotta get diagnosed. And so I got him diagnosed, you know, at the same time, went through the same process, went to a natural doctor, got him diagnosed. So he was diagnosed. So, and at the time, once Aiden's diagnosis came through, I started looking at ABA services. Well, at the time in Michigan, ABA wasn't covered under insurance. So, and it was\u8212\'97 there wasn't all these ABA centers around like there is today. So I inquired about it. And they said, well, it's gonna cost you about $30,000 a year out of pocket.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:06:38\u8211\'9600:06:50]\line So that's crazy. And like I said, at the time, insurances in Michigan didn't cover it. So we're like, well, this is beyond my own\u8212\'97 my means, you know, I don't have I don't have $30 grand.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:06:50\u8211\'9600:06:51]\line $30 grand sitting around to\u8212\'97}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:06:51\u8211\'9600:07:30]\line Sit around just to fork over for 1 year of ABA services. So I took what I could get as far as therapies, what I could find at the time. And so we did that for quite a few years. And then probably fast forward to 2019, my wife and I were having a lot of serious issues at that point and we got divorced. Eventually got divorced. I'll get into that. But we separated in September of 2019. The boys switched schools from Oxford to where they're presently at right now.\line Okay.\line And so I'm not like near Oxford or anything. I'm more closer. I'm in like suburban Detroit right now. So that was a big transition for the boys. And they were really traumatized from it because again, they're autistic and they can only understand certain concepts.\line Many children don't understand the concept of divorce, but when you put that on an autistic child, Mm-hmm. the trauma's gonna be there. And that kind of goes towards, like I was saying in a lot of my posts, where autism care and trauma do go hand in hand. You know, they were wondering why we weren't together anymore. They were wondering why we had 2 separate homes.\line which it was like at the time, we were in 2 separate homes, you know, they were living in one home part of the time, they were living at my, at their mother's place.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:08:21\u8211\'9600:08:44]\line So I just want, I just want a quick question here, Steve. And that is, so it sounds like both boys, again, they were diagnosed, but sounds like they were able to gain some functionality to communicate, like, so they've been, they've been able to speak to a certain degree. Would you say they have limited vocabulary or, you know, communication skills? Or, you know, because it sounds like they didn't, that they were You said they were confused about the living situation, which is understandable.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:08:44\u8211\'9600:08:46]\line Yeah, I'm sorry, fast forward to that.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:08:46\u8211\'9600:08:47]\line But yeah, no, it's okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:08:47\u8211\'9600:09:15]\line Yeah. But back to when they were in elementary school, I want to say by the time they hit kindergarten, they were starting to get somewhat verbal. Now, my youngest son, Aiden, was more verbal at a younger age than Matthew was. Matthew didn't start becoming verbal till about 5 years old. So that's around the time he started saying his first words. Um, eventually we were able to get into very basic conversations with both of them.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:09:15\u8211\'9600:09:16]\line Okay. Okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:09:16\u8211\'9600:09:18]\line Yeah. So, okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:09:18\u8211\'9600:09:46]\line So please continue. So, so that's the background. So, you know, again, in 2019, it's gotta be so hard. I mean, again, your relationships are challenging. I mean, that's a, that's a whole nother conversation I can have with so many people talking about balancing and managing Marriages, relationships when you're dealing with one, let alone 2 autistic kids. So you guys, you know, going through these challenges and you're trying to manage the trauma of changing school districts.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:09:46\u8211\'9600:10:28]\line Yeah. Yeah. So we went through that. So that's in September 2019. And my youngest son, Aiden, was having meltdowns at school just because he was unable to cope with the changes at the time. He wanted to go back to his old school, didn't want to go to where he was presently going. Matthew was also still having some issues. He wasn't having any meltdowns, but he was acting very hyper in the classroom and he wasn't even focusing. So let's fast forward to 3 months later. Yeah, probably maybe not 3 months, maybe more like 4 to 5 months later, COVID happens.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:10:28\u8211\'9600:10:34]\line Right, right, right. Yeah. Because you're going\u8212\'97 yeah, we're going from 2019, obviously, into early 2020. Yeah, COVID hits. Good times.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:10:34\u8211\'9600:10:43]\line COVID hits. So we're all in lockdown. You know, we can't go anywhere, kids can't go to school, we're going to\u8212\'97 they're going to Zoom, they're going to school over Zoom.\line Yeah.\line And, you know, they're wondering what's going on here. And so I ended up getting in touch with a therapist for both my boys, because all the stuff they've been through at that point. Well, again, during the lockdown sessions, they're over Zoom.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:11:00\u8211\'9600:11:01]\line Yeah.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:11:01\u8211\'9600:11:09]\line You try to do therapy with an autistic child over Zoom. For some, it might work. I don't think it really worked for my boys, really, to be honest with you.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:11:09\u8211\'9600:11:11]\line Yeah, it's hard. It's a challenge for sure.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:11:11\u8211\'9600:11:46]\line It is. It was. And so eventually, you know, lockdowns, you know, we eased back into the public school setting once things are opening up. My youngest son, my\u8212\'97 both my sons at this point were going to middle school. And so I actually, which was really fortunate because by this point COVID happened, they had a break from everything, from being on lockdown, which is ironic as it sounds. I was actually kind of grateful for that, you know, everything they've been through.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:11:46\u8211\'9600:11:49]\line Right, right. Kind of paused everything in a way.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
