David Kaufer}{
 [00:51:40\u8211\'9600:51:46]\line You want to elaborate on that a little bit in terms of\u8212\'97 so You see that perception still out there, don't you?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:51:46\u8211\'9600:52:14]\line Oh, yeah. Yeah, I do see it. Yeah. And, but not as much anymore. But I used to see it back early on when my kids were first diagnosed. But it's still out there. I can tell you from my own experiences, like my kids laugh, they make jokes, you know, that they're\u8212\'97 they show like if they're happy or if they're sad. You know, they show a whole array of emotions just like you and I do.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:52:14\u8211\'9600:52:59]\line Yeah. Yeah. I mean, the bottom line is, right, they're human first and autistic second. I mean, that's just, it's just, it's a, just, it's a truly just a word we use to describe kind of this, you know, the way their brains are wired a little differently and how it expresses itself differently too. Um, like you say, yeah, they still have emotions.\line That's, that's, that's a great one. All right, the next one. And again, this is one that this whole episode has been kind of like a parent confession. But we like to talk about things that, you know, things that we've learned that we can share with others that maybe either make us cringe a little bit, or again, maybe, you know, makes us\u8212\'97 hindsight, we're like, God, I can't believe I did that. So we've, we've got a little jingle for that as well.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:52:59\u8211\'9600:53:33]\line Morning routines can go astray. Found socks in the fridge one day. Laughter's the best remedy. Hey, we're just finding our own way. Mom-Dad Confessions every week, sharing joys and moments we seek. Honest tales that make us unique.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:53:37\u8211\'9600:53:48]\line Yeah, and it is a club, right? Like, we all\u8212\'97 it's a parenting club, and then there's the autism parenting club. It's, you know, we all have things that we look back at, like I said, and just kind of say, what, what was I thinking?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Stephen Laine}{
 [00:53:48\u8211\'9600:54:22]\line Yeah. Um, as far as like me, like, I could, you know, look back at things, you know, like when my kids were first diagnosed, and I didn't believe it. And it took me a year to come to her diagnosis. I didn't come to church for\u8212\'97 it took me a full year. And so back then, I didn't even tell anybody. I didn't tell family. I didn't tell my closest friends. I didn't tell anybody. And so I didn't come out until 2012 and say, hey, my kids have autism. Even my own mother didn't know.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
